# Which stored integers hold an address into a memory file's arena.
#
# Derived by tests/check-vm-address-fields.sh from every structure the
# library declares, so a member added anywhere lands here without a
# disposition and the build says so. Two dispositions:
#
#   address  an offset into an arena. A pass that rearranged the arena --
#            compacting it so the live blocks sit together and the tail can
#            be handed back -- must rewrite every one of these. Each carries
#            the reason it is one.
#   count    anything else: a size, an index, an entity number, a counter, a
#            flag, a handle, a generation, a coordinate, a host pointer. Not
#            affected by where the bytes of an entity sit.
#
# The dispositions are not taken on trust: the check also derives which
# members reach the offset argument of a call that turns one into a pointer,
# and a member filed as a count that gets there is reported. That is a floor
# under the register rather than a substitute for it, since a member reached
# through a subscript -- the table row -- does not match.
#
# The address column is short because virtual memory is referenced by entity
# number almost everywhere, and the table is what turns a number into a
# place. The exceptions are the stacks and the two arrays an operator record
# points at, which are named by where they are.
#
# disposition  header  member  [reason, for an address]
address  xpost_context.h        ds                       the dictionary stack, same
address  xpost_context.h        es                       the execution stack, same
address  xpost_context.h        hold                     the stack a constructor stashes a new object on until its operator returns, same
address  xpost_context.h        os                       the operand stack, named by where it is rather than by which entity it is
address  xpost_memory.h         adr                      where an entity's bytes begin, and the one every other reference is turned into a place through
address  xpost_memory.h         free_substack            a parked save-record stack, held by where it is because those stacks are raw file allocations rather than table entities
address  xpost_memory.h         used                     the cursor the arena is handed out from, which a rearrangement would set to the new high-water rather than move
address  xpost_name.h           eq                       the subtree for a matching character, EXCEPT at a terminator node -- where val is TST_END -- at which it holds the name's index on the name stack instead, so a pass that rewrote it unconditionally would corrupt every name's payload
address  xpost_name.h           hi                       the subtree of the name tree for keys above this node's, held as an address
address  xpost_name.h           lo                       the subtree of the name tree for keys below this node's, likewise
address  xpost_object.h         stk                      the record stack a save object owns, reached with xpost_stack_at and so a place rather than a number
address  xpost_operator.h       sigadr                   the array of an operator's signatures, in global virtual memory
address  xpost_operator.h       t                        the array of argument types one of those signatures names, likewise
address  xpost_stack.h          nextseg                  the next segment of a chained stack, so a moved segment would orphan the rest of the chain
address  xpost_stack.h          prevseg                  the previous segment, and on the root segment the top one; same
